The value of $\tan A$ will be maximum when $BC$ is perpendicular to $\overline{AC}$</think>
Answer:
[asy]
import olympiad;
size(5cm);
defaultpen(linewidth(0.7)+fontsize(11pt));
pair A=origin, B=(15,0), C=(15,20), P=foot(C,B,A);
draw (A--B--C--cycle);
draw(rightanglemark(B,P,C));
label("A", A, SW);
label("B", B, S);
label("C", C, NE);
label("P", P, NW);
label("15", (A+B)/2, S);
label("20", (A+C)/2, E);
[/asy]
$BC$ must be perpendicular to $\overline{AC}$ since $AB$ is shorter than $BC$, so by the Pythagorean Theorem,
\[AC = \sqrt{20^2-15} = \sqrt{275}.\]Therefore, $\tan A = \frac{BC}{AC} = \frac{15}{\sqrt{275}}=\frac{3\sqrt{11}}{11},$ so our answer is $\boxed{\frac{3\sqrt{11}}{11}}.$

